y61Wzg law.medarbejdere.au.dk Session selvbetjening sbstads.au.dk Session X-SurveyIT-HAProxy-surveyxact_frontend survey.au.dk Session esctx-nfHdeloMxho .login.microsoftonline.com Session __Secure-ROLLOUT_TOKEN .youtube.com 5 months 4 weeks msal.cache.encryption forms.cloud.microsoft Session Queue-it-audk________________audkprod2026 audk.queue-it.net 1 day productCode soeg.kb.dk Session __Secure-UqZBpD3n3naPU20-9Fvn5i-TSO9FpchbYtbA9YCEpg3UXgo_ soeg.kb.dk 1 year esctx-O2uzNWDgQPY .login.microsoftonline.com Session esctx-cnOtbYn1wEE .login.microsoftonline.com Session What is a cookie? A cookie is a text file, that is stored on your computer, mobile or similar device. When visiting our website it is sent forth and back between your browser and a web server. It is used by AU or a third party, which is described more specific under the cookie descriptions at the bottom of this page. Cookies cannot contain malicious code such as virus. Cookies can be either temporary or permanent. The temporary cookies are linked to one specific visit, and are deleted again when you close the website. The permanent cookie is saved on your computer, mobile or tablet for a period of time. How long the various cookies remain is described in the cookie descriptions at the bottom of the page. Each time you visit the website, however, the cookie will be renewed and the period will thus start all over again. What data is collected via cookies? Most cookies collect anonymous data, such as the amount of time of your visit to the website, what pages you have visited, whether your computer, tablet or smartphone has used the pages before, which website you came from, which browser and operating system you use and the like. In some cases, IP addresses are also collected, which are personally identifiable data. Therefore, you can also read our Privacy Policy , which tells how we at AU process personal information. If any of the third party services, we use, process data on behalf of AU, we will, if possible, enter into a Data Processing Agreement with them. The supplier is thus not allowed to use the data for their own purposes or to pass them on to others. Delete cookies It is also possible to delete or block cookies. See the guide: https://www.allaboutcookies.org/manage-cookies/clear-cookies-installed.html The website is owned and published by: Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 DK-8000 Aarhus C Tel. no: 87 15 00 00 Email: au@au.dk Cookies consent ID : Cookie report created by CookieScript"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoc-position-within-the-engineering-and-production-of-catalytic-bacterial-amyloids-in-bacterial-biofilm": {
      "title": "Postdoc position within the engineering and production of catalytic bacterial amyloids in bacterial biofilm",
      "description": "Postdoc position within the engineering and production of catalytic bacterial amyloids in bacterial biofilm - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU Danish website You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability Campus development History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Protein Science - Gustav Wieds Vej Postdoc position within the engineering and production of catalytic bacterial amyloids in bacterial biofilm Deadline 7 Sep 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Nov Protein Science - Gustav Wieds Vej Gustav Wieds Vej 14 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Nov 2026 - 31 Jan 2030 ID: 166609 Apply We are seeking applicants for a 39-month postdoctoral position in Daniel Otzen’s research group at iNANO, Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics at Aarhus University. Expected start date and duration of employment 1 November 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. Job description We invite applicants for a postdoctoral position in the Horizon Europe Pathfinder project CATAMPLAS, where you will help pioneer next-generation catalytic biomaterials based on engineered bacterial functional amyloids. Working at the interface of microbiology, protein science, and biotechnology, you will investigate how bacterial biofilm proteins can be overexpressed and engineered into sustainable catalytic materials for plastic degradation. The position offers a unique opportunity to collaborate within an interdisciplinary European consortium comprising leading expertise in microbial engineering, protein engineering, enzymology, structural and biophysical characterization, biomaterials, industrial biotechnology, and technology validation. Your profile Applicants must hold a PhD in microbiology, molecular biology, biochemistry, biotechnology, bioengineering, or a related discipline. We are seeking a highly motivated researcher with a strong publication record and documented experience relevant to microbial protein engineering. The successful candidate should have: Hands-on experience with bacterial genetics, molecular cloning, recombinant protein expression, and microbial cultivation. Expertise in bacterial biofilms, functional amyloids, or extracellular protein assemblies. Experience with protein purification and biochemical characterization. Excellent written and spoken English and the ability to work effectively in an interdisciplinary and international research environment. The following qualifications will be considered an advantage: Experience with protein biophysics (e.g., spectroscopy or protein assembly characterization) and/or enzymology. Experience with synthetic biology, protein engineering, or microbial cell factories. Strong collaborative skills, scientific curiosity, independence, and the ability to drive research while contributing to a multidisciplinary European consortium. Who we are Work will be carried out in the group of Daniel Otzen at the iNANO House within the Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics at Aarhus University. As part of the Horizon Europe Pathfinder project CATAMPLAS, you will collaborate closely with leading European research groups specializing in protein engineering, enzymology, microbial synthetic biology, biomaterials, biophysical characterization, plastic degradation, and technology translation. The consortium combines complementary expertise spanning computational protein design, microbial cell factories, catalytic biomaterials, polymer science, and industrial validation, providing a unique environment for interdisciplinary research and innovation. Daniel Otzen’s research focuses on the structure, assembly, and function of bacterial functional amyloids and their engineering for novel biotechnological applications. Our group has made seminal contributions to understanding the molecular mechanisms governing functional amyloid formation, stability, and interactions, and has pioneered the use of engineered bacterial amyloids as programmable protein scaffolds with catalytic functionality. More recently, we have developed catalytic amyloids capable of promoting plastic hydrolysis, providing a foundation for the CATAMPLAS project. We foster a collaborative, curiosity-driven research culture where independence, creativity, and open scientific exchange are highly valued, and where early-career researchers are encouraged to develop their own scientific ideas while working closely with leading international collaborators. We offer an exciting and friendly interdisciplinary research environment with extensive national, international, and industrial collaborations through the Horizon Europe Pathfinder project CATAMPLAS with leading European experts in protein engineering, enzymology, microbial synthetic biology, biomaterials, polymer science, and technology translation a research climate that encourages curiosity, scientific independence, and lively, open, and critical discussions where early-career researchers are encouraged to develop their own scientific ideas a workplace characterized by professionalism, equality, diversity, a healthy work–life balance, close working relationships, networking opportunities and mentoring Place of work and area of employment The place of work is Gustav Wieds Vej 14, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark, and the area of employment is Aarhus University. Contact information For further information, please contact: Professor Daniel Otzen, dao@inano.au.dk Deadline Applications must be received no later than 7 September 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Daniel Erik Otzen Professor, Department of Molecular Biology and Genetics - Protein Science - Gustav Wieds Vej +4520725238 dao@mbg.au.dk Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 nat-tech.HR.team1@au.dk Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday  7 Sep 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: au@au.dk Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48 English Danish Cookies improve user experience When you click 'Accept all' cookies, Aarhus University can give you the best user experience. Cookies store information about how a user interacts with a website. All your data is anonymised and cannot be used to identify you. You can always change your consent again under ‘Cookies' in the website footer. The university uses its own cookies and cookies set by our partners. Strictly necessary Statistic Targeting Functionality Unclassified Save & Close Accept all Decline all Read more about cookies Hide details List of cookies Cookie Policy Strictly necessary Statistic Targeting Functionality Unclassified These cookies make it possible to use basic website functionality, e.g. navigation etc. The website does not work without these cookies. Cookie report Name Provider / Domain Expires Description be_typo_user TYPO3 Association .au.dk 30 minutes This cookie is set by our CMS provider; TYPO3 and is used to identify a backend session when a Backend User is logged in to TYPO3 Backend or Frontend. fe_typo_user Typo3 Association .au.dk 30 minutes This cookie is associated with the Typo3 web content management system. It is generally used as a user session identifier to enable user preferences to be stored, but in many cases it may not actually be needed as it can be set by default by the platform, though this can be prevented by site administrators. In most cases it is set to be destroyed at the end of a browser session. It contains a random identifier rather than any specific user data. ASP.NET_SessionId Microsoft Corporation .au.dk Session General purpose platform session co