      "title": "Student worker with an interest in AI, learning technology, and quantitative methods",
      "description": "Student worker with an interest in AI, learning technology, and quantitative methods - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU Danish website You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability Campus development History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Student worker with an interest in AI, learning technology, and quantitative methods Deadline 15 Sep 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Nov Fixed term part-time position 1 Nov 2026 - 31 Oct 2027 9 hours per week ID: 168026 Apply Are you analytically sharp and interested in how artificial intelligence can support learning and teaching? Then we have an exciting opportunity for you at Aarhus BSS. We are looking for a student programmer for a research and development project on agentic AI companions, learning analytics, and data-driven educational innovation in Business Intelligence courses. About the job As a student programmer, you will be affiliated with a research and development project on the use of agentic AI companions in Business Intelligence courses at Aarhus BSS. The project develops, tests, and evaluates AI-based learning assistants that can support students in accessing course materials, receiving feedback, and organising their learning process. The aim is to examine how responsible use of AI can contribute to more adaptive, personalised, and data-informed learning experiences. The project combines educational development, generative AI tools, learning analytics, experimental implementation, and quantitative analysis of learning outcomes, student satisfaction, workload, and system interactions. You will become part of an academically stimulating project environment and gain hands-on experience with the use of AI and data analysis in a real educational setting. As our new student programmer, your tasks will include: assisting with the development, testing, and documentation of AI-based learning tools, including AI companions for course materials, feedback, and study support helping to structure and adapt course materials so they can be used in AI-based learning environments collecting, cleaning, and structuring data from surveys, teaching activities, and, when relevant, system logs contributing to quantitative analyses of learning outcomes, satisfaction, workload, and interaction with AI tools preparing reports, figures, and visualisations of the project results assisting with literature searches and summaries of research on AI in education, adaptive learning, self-regulated learning, and learning analytics collaborating with the project team to ensure that the insights are relevant, useful, and well documented. About you We expect you to have an interest in AI and educational technology, and to be motivated to contribute to a research project with both practical and academic ambitions. You do not need to be an expert in all areas, but you should be curious, structured, and willing to learn. We imagine that you: have experience with quantitative methods and data management have experience with programming, for example in Python, R, or similar have an interest in AI, generative AI, learning technology, or business analytics preferably have experience with survey data, experimental designs, text data, or visualisations can work independently, in a structured way, and with attention to detail are enrolled in a relevant higher education programme, for example business intelligence, psychology, education, data science, information systems, business analytics, economics, or similar Experience with generative AI tools, prompt engineering, API use, learning analytics, or experimental methods is an advantage, but not a requirement. Some tasks will involve programming, especially in relation to testing AI-based tools, supporting retrieval-augmented generation setups, and assisting with data collection and analysis. However, the position also includes non-programming tasks such as literature searches, documentation, preparation of teaching materials, and reporting. Do you have any questions? If you have questions about the positions, you are welcome to contact the researcher responsible for the project: Associate Professor Ana Alina Tudoran, anat@econ.au.dk. If you need help with uploading your job application, please contact Office Apprentice Femke Wiltink per email: fw@au.dk. Terms of employment The position as student programmer is a fixed-term employment for 12 months, starting on 1 November 2026. The workplace is Aarhus BSS, Aarhus University. The working time is expected to be approximately 9 hours on average per week. There will be an opportunity for flexible organization of working hours by agreement with the immediate supervisor, including in connection with exam periods. Application If you wish to be considered for the position, please send a short motivated application, CV, relevant documentation of your current education, and any documentation of previous relevant employment and other qualifications. Deadline: 15 September 2026 In your application, you are welcome to briefly describe your experience with data analysis, programming, AI tools, or education-related projects. Aarhus University aims to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for everyone and seeks a culture where each individual can thrive and develop. We regard equality and diversity as a strength and therefore encourage all interested candidates to apply. Løn- og ansættelsesvilkår Løn- og ansættelsesvilkår sker i henhold til organisationsaftale mellem Finansministeriet og PROSA/STAT IT-medarbejder (PROSA) i statens tjeneste samt til den en hver tid gældende lokalaftale. Du indplaceres i løngruppe 1-5 afhængig af uddannelse og kvalifikationer. Aarhus Universitet Aarhus University aims to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for everyone and seeks a culture where each individual can thrive and develop. We regard equality and diversity as a strength and therefore encourage all interested candidates to apply. Ansøgning sendes via Aarhus Universitets rekrutteringssystem, som kan tilgås under stillingsopslaget på Aarhus Universitets hjemmeside. Questions about the position\n                            ? Ana Alina Tudoran Associate Professor, Department of Economics and Business Economics +4587165193 anat@econ.au.dk Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Aarhus BSS Administrative Centre - Aarhus BSS HR and PhD Administration +4587152173 emply.ar.hr@au.dk Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 15 Sep 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: au@au.dk Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48 English Danish Cookies improve user experience When you click 'Accept all' cookies, Aarhus University can give you the best user experience. Cookies store information about how a user interacts with a website. All your data is anonymised and cannot be used to identify you. You can always change your consent again under ‘Cookies' in the website footer. The university uses its own cookies and cookies set by our partners. Strictly necessary Statistic Targeting Functionality Unclassified Save & Close Accept all Decline all Read more about cookies Hide details List of cookies Cookie Policy Strictly necessary Statistic Targeting Functionality Unclassified These cookies make it possible to use basic website functionality, e.g. navigation etc. The website does not work without these cookies. Cookie report Name Provider / Domain Expires Description be_typo_user TYPO3 Association .au.dk 30 minutes This cookie is set by our CMS provider; TYPO3 and is used to identify a backend session when a Backend User is logged in to TYPO3 Backend or Frontend. fe_typo_user Typo3 Association .au.dk 30 minutes This cookie is associated with the Typo3 web content management system. It is generally used as a user session identifier to enable user preferences to be stored, but in many cases it may not actually be needed as it can be set by default by the platform, though this can be prevented by site administrators. In most cases it is set to be destroyed at the end of a browser session. It contains a random identifier rather than any specific user data. ASP.NET_SessionId Microsoft Corporation .au.dk Session General purpose platform session cookie, used by sites written with Miscrosoft .NET based technologies. Usually used to maintain an anonymised user session by the server. JSESSIONID Oracle Corporation .au.dk Session General purpose platform session cookie, used by sites written in JSP. Usually used to maintain an anonymous user session by the server. ARRAffinity Microsoft Corporation .mitstudie.au.dk Session This cookie is set by websites run on the Windows Azure cloud platform. It is used for load balancing to make sure the visitor page requests are routed to the same server in any browsing session. esctx Microsoft Corporati