    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/research-assistant-for-chromatin-perturbation-in-neurons": {
      "title": "Research Assistant for chromatin perturbation in neurons",
      "description": "Research Assistant for chromatin perturbation in neurons  - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU Danish website You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability Campus development History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biomedicine - Forskning og uddannelse, Skou-bygningen - Faculty: Health Sciences Research Assistant for chromatin perturbation in neurons Deadline 15 Sep 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Oct Department of Biomedicine - Forskning og uddannelse, Skou-bygningen Høegh-Guldbergs Gade 10 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Oct 2026 - 31 Mar 2027 ID: 168085 Apply The Department of Biomedicine at the Faculty of Health at Aarhus University invites applications for a position as a Research Assistant to study chromatin perturbation in neurons as per 1 October 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. The position is a fixed-term full-time position for six months. The department of Biomedicine prioritises diversity and a good work environment, as this is a prerequisite for groundbreaking research. In a diverse and international research environment, dedicated employees are looking to generate new knowledge within biomedical research areas such as infection and inflammation, membranes, neuroscience and personalised medicine. The Department of Biomedicine provides research-based teaching of the highest quality and is responsible for a large part of the medical degree programme. Academic staff contribute to the teaching. English is the preferred language in the laboratory, at meetings and at seminars. The department employs approx. 500 people from all over the world, and they make use of the department's modern laboratory-, core- and animal facilities. The Department of Biomedicine focuses on innovation, entrepreneurship and collaboration with business and industry, and numerous researchers from the department have established companies to develop new medicinal treatments founded in professional scientific basic research. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . Your job responsibilities As a Research Assistant, your primary tasks are to contribute to cutting-edge research to uncover the chromatin-based mechanisms that regulate gene expression and cellular memory in neurons. You contribute to the development of the department through interdisciplinary research of high international quality. In your daily work, you have a close interaction with competent colleagues. You will combine molecular biology, neuroscience, epigenomics, stem cell biology, genomics, and computational biology. You are expected to actively contribute to ongoing research projects while helping establish new experimental and analytical approaches to investigate chromatin dynamics in neuronal systems. In your daily work, you will collaborate closely with researchers, students, and technical staff in an international and highly collaborative research environment. You will also help maintain high standards of scientific rigor, reproducibility, and laboratory organization. Your main tasks will consist of: Culture and maintain human induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and hiPSC-derived neuronal cells, including neuronal differentiation and characterization. Perform a broad range of molecular and cellular biology techniques, including immunocytochemistry, fluorescence microscopy, DNA/RNA extraction, PCR, qRT-PCR, and Western blotting, as appropriate. Generate and validate lentiviral vectors for genetic manipulation and functional studies in hiPSCs and hiPSC-derived neuronal cells. Generate high-quality samples and sequencing libraries for chromatin-related genomic and epigenomic assays, including single-cell multi-omics and other next-generation sequencing applications. Perform quality control and contribute to the analysis, interpretation, and documentation of experimental and multi-omics data. Maintain accurate and comprehensive experimental records, organize and manage research data. You will report to Professor Kyung Min Noh. Your competences You have a background within molecular biology, epigenetics, neuroscience, genomics, or a closely related discipline. Previous research experience with developing a single-cell omics platform is highly desirable. As a person, you have good interpersonal skills, are inclusive and team-oriented and able to contribute to a good work environment. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. In order to be assessed as qualified for the position, you must meet these academic criteria . Shortlisting will be used. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Kyung Min Noh tel.: (+45) 22487695. Your place of work will be the Department of Biomedicine, Building 1115 and 1116, Høegh-Guldbergs Gade 10, C. F. Møllers Allé 6, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. We expect to conduct interviews week 39. Terms of employment Appointment as a research assistant requires academic qualifications at master's degree level. Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial Order on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities . Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - other academic positions A list of publications can be uploaded if relevant for the position A teaching portfolio can be uploaded if relevant for the position. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the recruitment system We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Kyung Min Noh Professor, Department of Biomedicine - Forskning og uddannelse, Skou-bygningen mnoh@biomed.au.dk Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4520874354 emply.he.hr@au.dk Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 15 Sep 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: au@au.dk Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48 English Danish Cookies improve user experience When you click 'Accept all' cookies, Aarhus University can give you the best user experience. Cookies store information about how a user interacts with a website. All your data is anonymised and cannot be used to identify you. You can always change your consent again under ‘Cookies' in the website footer. The university uses its own cookies and cookies set by our partners. Strictly necessary Statistic Targeting Functionality Unclassified Save & Close Accept all Decline all Read more about cookies Hide details List of cookies Cookie Policy Strictly necessary Statistic Targeting Functionality Unclassified These cookies make it possible to use basic website functionality, e.g. navigation etc. The website does not work without these cookies. Cookie report Name Provider / Domain Expires Description be_typo_user TYPO3 Association .au.dk 30 minutes This cookie is set by our CMS provider; TYPO3 and is used to identify a backend session when a Backend User is logged in to TYPO3 Backend or Frontend. fe_typo_user Typo3 Association .au.dk 30 minutes This cookie is associated with the Typo3 web content management system. It is generally used as a user session identifier to enable user preferences to be stored, but in many cases it may not actually be needed as it can be set by default by the platform, though this can be prevented by site administrators. In most cases it is set to be destroyed at the end of a browser session. It contains a random identifier rather than any specific user data. ASP.NET_SessionId Microsoft Corporation .au.dk Session General purpose platform session cookie, used by sites written with Miscrosoft .NET based technologies. Usually used to maintain an anonymised user session by the server. JSESSIONID Oracle Corporation .au.dk Session General purpose platform session cookie, used by sites written in JSP. Usually used to maintain an anonymous 