ctx-nfHdeloMxho .login.microsoftonline.com Session __Secure-ROLLOUT_TOKEN .youtube.com 5 months 4 weeks msal.cache.encryption forms.cloud.microsoft Session Queue-it-audk________________audkprod2026 audk.queue-it.net 1 day productCode soeg.kb.dk Session __Secure-UqZBpD3n3naPU20-9Fvn5i-TSO9FpchbYtbA9YCEpg3UXgo_ soeg.kb.dk 1 year esctx-O2uzNWDgQPY .login.microsoftonline.com Session esctx-cnOtbYn1wEE .login.microsoftonline.com Session What is a cookie? A cookie is a text file, that is stored on your computer, mobile or similar device. When visiting our website it is sent forth and back between your browser and a web server. It is used by AU or a third party, which is described more specific under the cookie descriptions at the bottom of this page. Cookies cannot contain malicious code such as virus. Cookies can be either temporary or permanent. The temporary cookies are linked to one specific visit, and are deleted again when you close the website. The permanent cookie is saved on your computer, mobile or tablet for a period of time. How long the various cookies remain is described in the cookie descriptions at the bottom of the page. Each time you visit the website, however, the cookie will be renewed and the period will thus start all over again. What data is collected via cookies? Most cookies collect anonymous data, such as the amount of time of your visit to the website, what pages you have visited, whether your computer, tablet or smartphone has used the pages before, which website you came from, which browser and operating system you use and the like. In some cases, IP addresses are also collected, which are personally identifiable data. Therefore, you can also read our Privacy Policy , which tells how we at AU process personal information. If any of the third party services, we use, process data on behalf of AU, we will, if possible, enter into a Data Processing Agreement with them. The supplier is thus not allowed to use the data for their own purposes or to pass them on to others. Delete cookies It is also possible to delete or block cookies. See the guide: https://www.allaboutcookies.org/manage-cookies/clear-cookies-installed.html The website is owned and published by: Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 DK-8000 Aarhus C Tel. no: 87 15 00 00 Email: au@au.dk Cookies consent ID : Cookie report created by CookieScript"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/academic-position-with-focus-on-bioinformatic-and-computational-support": {
      "title": "Academic position with focus on bioinformatic and computational support",
      "description": "Academic position with focus on bioinformatic and computational support - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biology - Genetics, Ecology and Evolution Academic position with focus on bioinformatic and computational support Deadline 17 Sep 23:59 CEST Expected start 15 May Department of Biology - Genetics, Ecology and Evolution Ny Munkegade 114 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 15 May 2027 - 14 May 2029 ID: 168197 Apply Applications are invited for a 2-year Academic position in the field of evolutionary genomics and bioinformatics at the Department of Biology, Aarhus University, Denmark. Expected start date and duration of employment This is a fixed-term 2-year position with a start the 15 May 2027 or as soon as possible hereafter. Job description The position will focus on providing bioinformatic and computational support for the processing, analysis, management, and interpretation of genomic data, thereby supporting scientific staff in their research activities and collaborative projects: Supporting the processing and quality control of whole-genome sequencing and resequencing data, ensuring that datasets are suitable for downstream scientific analyses. Supporting the development and the performance of statistical analysis of genomic and ecological data Supporting de novo genome assembly and genome annotation, including the implementation, maintenance, and optimization of relevant bioinformatic workflows. Supporting scientific staff with genomic analyses of individual and pooled resequencing data, including data preparation, computational analyses, quality control, and generation of results for further scientific interpretation. Developing, adapting, and maintaining bioinformatic pipelines and computational workflows to facilitate efficient and reproducible analysis of large genomic datasets. Assisting with the organization, management, documentation, and quality control of genomic datasets and analysis workflows. Providing technical and computational support to students, PhD students, postdoctoral researchers, and faculty members working with genomic data. Supporting the integration and preparation of genomic data with demographic, ecological, and environmental datasets for subsequent scientific analyses by the research team. Contributing to the reproducibility and documentation of computational analyses, including maintaining scripts, workflows, and analysis records. Assisting with the visualization, summarization, and preparation of genomic results for use by scientific staff in publications, reports, presentations, and collaborative research activities. Contributing to collaborative research projects by providing bioinformatic expertise and technical support throughout the different stages of genomic data analysis. You will be involved in collaborative research projects as well as independent projects, and will work in close collaboration with students, PhD students, post docs and other faculty members. Many research activities occur in close collaboration with colleagues and members of Centre for Ecological Genetics at the Section for Genetics, Ecology and Evolution. Many research activities occur in close collaboration with colleagues and members of Centre for Ecological Genetics at the Section for Genetics, Ecology and Evolution. Your profile Applicants should hold a relevant Master’s degree. A PhD in evolutionary genomics or a related field is considered an advantage. Strong profile and background in evolutionary genetics, genomics, population genetics, bioinformatics. Extensive experience in bioinformatic analyses, coding and development of pipelines, handling of large genomic data sets, experience with population genetic and evolutionary analyses. Demonstrated ability of being able to support researchers in completing research projects and preparing manuscripts for publication. Open to and engaged in collaborative projects and supervision of students Who we are The Section for Genetics, Ecology at the Department of Biology performs evolutionary research on a broad range of questions and systems and provides a strong and international research environment. The Centre for Ecological Genetics provides a strong and vibrant research environment in ecological genomics, landscape genomics, evolutionary genetics and population genomics. The work environment is open and collaborative with many academic and social activities. What we offer The department/centre offers: a well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national and international collaborators and researchers at all levels a research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. Place of work and area of employment The place of work is the Department of Biology, Section for Genetics, Ecology & Evolution, Centre for Ecological Genetics: Aarhus University Ny Munkegade 116 DK - 8000 Aarhus C Contact information For further information, please contact: Prof. Trine Bilde, E-mail: [email protected] Formalities and salary range Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions. You will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Trine Bilde Professor, Centre Director, Department of Biology - Genetics, Ecology and Evolution +4560202702 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522521469 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Thursday 17 Sep 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoc-in-personality-disorders-research": {
      "title": "Postdoc in Personality Disorders Research",
      "description": "Postdoc in Personality Disorders Research  - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Affective Disorders - Faculty: Health Sciences Postdoc in Personality Disorders Research Deadline 22 Sep 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Mar Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Affective Disorders Palle Juul-Jensens Boulevard 175 8200 Aarhus N Fixed term full-time position 1 Mar 2027 - 28 Feb 2030 ID: 168572 Apply The Department of Clinical Medicine at Faculty of Health at Aarhus University invites applications for a position as Postdoc in the field of Affective Disorders as per 1 March 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The position is a fixed-term full-time position for 3 years. As a postdoc at the Department of Clinical Medicine, you will be part of what is probably the largest health science research department in Denmark. Our clinical research covers all the medical specialities and takes place in close collaboration with the university hospital and the regional hospitals in the Central Denmark Region. We have approx. 30,000 square metres of modern research facilities for experimental surgery and medicine, animal facilities and also advanced scanners at our disposal. The department has overall responsibility for the Master's degree programs in medicine and in molecular medicine. At the department we are approx. 670 academic employees, 500 PhD students and 160 technical/administrative employees who are cooperating across disciplines. As a postdoc, you will be working at Aarhus University Hospital or another hospital in the Central Denmark Region. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . Place of work Your place of work will be the Department of Affective Disorders (ADA) at Aarhus University and Aarhus University Hospital-Psychiatry. ADA is a specialized clinical and research department focused on the diagnosis, treatment, and understanding of mood, anxiety and personality-related psychiatric conditions. The department integrates neuroscience, psychology, epidemiology and pharmacology to explore the biological and psychological mechanisms underlying affective disorders, contributing to improved therapeutic strategies and patient outcomes. With a strong emphasis on interdisciplinary collaboration and international partnerships, ADA plays a pivotal role in advancing mental health care in Denmark and beyond. ADA operates under the Department of Clinical Medicine (IKM) at Aarhus University - one of Denmark’s leading institutions for clinical research and medical education. Researchers in IKM conduct research across all medical specialties—including psychiatry, cancer, cardiology, neuroscience, and infectious diseases—in close collaboration with Aarhus University Hospital and regional hospitals in the Central Denmark Region. The department is home to over 350 researchers and nearly 500 PhD students, fostering a vibrant and inclusive research culture. With a strong emphasis on transparency, knowledge sharing, and equity, IKM supports career development and interdisciplinary collaboration while providing access to cutting-edge facilities. About the research project The focus of this project is the epidemiology and shared genetics of eating disorders and personality (EpiGen-EP). Personality disorder (PD) characteristics are often present in patients with eating disorders (EDs), but we know little about the epidemiology or biological underpinnings of comorbid ED and PD. Using data from Denmark and Estonia, this project will examine the epidemiology and genetics of ED and disordered personality and identify meaningful subgroups for targeted treatment and prevention strategies. Remote work policy Our policy regarding remote work is that all members of the research group are expected to be present at the office on Monday, Wednesday and Thursday, and may work from home if they prefer on Tuesdays and Fridays. Extended periods of remote work up to 1 month are allowable with prior mutual agreement. Longer-term exceptions to the policy are negotiable under extraordinary circumstances. Data sources As part of this project, you will be working extensively with Danish national health registers and the iPSYCH sample. The Danish national health registers, overseen by the Danish Health Data Authority, are a comprehensive and centralized collection of health-related data covering the entire Danish population. These registers systematically record information from hospitals, pharmacies, and municipal health services, capturing data on diagnoses, treatments, medications, births, deaths, and more. With over 40 years of longitudinal data, including the extensive National Patient Register, these resources enable robust epidemiological and clinical research. Their unique infrastructure allows for secure linkage across databases, supporting high-quality, population-wide studies while maintaining strict privacy standards. iPSYCH is a national project founded in 2012 by six leading researchers in the fields of psychiatry and genetics. The purpose of iPSYCH is to find the causes and create the basis for better treatment and prevention of five of the most serious mental disorders: autism, ADHD, schizophrenia, bipolar disorder and depression. By examining genetic and risk factors in more than 130,000 Danes–both those who suffer from mental disorders and those who do not–iPSYCH sheds light on the interaction between heredity factors and the environment, which for some people result in them developing a mental disorder. Today iPSYCH is one of the world’s largest studies of the genetic and environmental causes of mental disorders and it involves more than 150 researchers within psychiatry, genetics and register-based research. Your job responsibilities Your primary task will be to conduct research of high international quality. Although the position is primarily research based, a small degree of teaching and supervision (in English) will be required. In your daily work, you will work closely with both Danish and international colleagues. In your day-to-day work, you will be supervised by Dr. Katherine Musliner, associate professor in the ADA research unit, and Dr. Zeynep Yilmaz, associate professor in the Department of Public Health at Aarhus University. Drs. Musliner and Yilmaz are co-leaders of the EpiGen-EP project. The position of postdoc  is a career development position in which you will receive supervision and career development opportunities. Your main tasks will consist of: Independent research of high international quality, including publication A limited amount of teaching and supervision Some degree of contribution towards attracting external research funding Contributions to internal and external efforts to disseminate research findings to relevant academic, clinical and patient stakeholder groups, including planning and carrying out workshops and symposia Participation in, and active contribution to, the development of the working environment of the research unit, including journal clubs and research seminars Administratively, you will report to the head of the Department of Affective Disorders research unit, Dr. Søren D. Østergaard. Your competences The ideal candidate for this position will have a strong background in psychiatric genetic epidemiology, personality disorders and/or eating disorders, data analysis and statistical modeling. Prior experience working with Danish registers is an advantage. As a person, the ideal candidate will have good interpersonal skills, will be inclusive and team-oriented and able to contribute to a good working environment. Our research unit is highly international, therefore cultural awareness, open communication, adaptability and mutual respect are highly valued. Qualification requirements are as follows: Education background at the PhD level in psychiatric epidemiology and/or psychiatric genetics, or a closely related field. Experience and proficiency working with large datasets (experience working with Danish national registers strongly preferred) Experience and proficiency with data analysis and statistical modeling Competence with at least one of the following statistical software packages: SAS, STATA or R Curiosity, internal motivation, a positive attitude and a strong love of science and scientific research Fluency in spoken and written English required. Additional proficiency in spoken and written Danish is an advantage. As a person, you have good interpersonal skills, are inclusive and team-oriented and able to contribute to a good work environment. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. In order to be assessed as qualified for a Postdoc position, you must meet these academic criteria . To be considered for the position, applicants must meet the formal requirements for academic appointments at Aarhus University and submit all required application materials by the deadline. Applications that are substantially incomplete will not proceed to further assessment Obtaining a child certificate is a prerequisite for employment in the position. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Associate Professor Katherine Musliner tel +45 5271 4661. Your place of work will be the Department of Clinical Medicine, Palle Juul-Jensens Boulevard 11, 8200 Aarhus, Denmark. Terms of employment Appointment as a postdoc requires academic qualifications at PhD level. Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . The yearly base salary for a fulltime postdoc is between DKK 493.394,35 and DKK 585.322,48 depending on the years of working experience after achieved MSc degree. The base salary includes a position related supplement and pension (18.07 %). Additional supplement(s) for special qualifications can be negotiated. Authorisation supplemement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Researchers recruited from abroad are offered a special researcher tax scheme with a lower tax rate. Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial Order on Job Structure for Academic Staff Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - postdoc A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios A maximum of five of the publications of greatest relevance to the job may be submitted (optional) Research plan can be uploaded (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the e-recruitment system (optional) We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including assistance with relocation and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information about the International Staff Office and the range of services here . Aarhus University also has a Junior Researcher Association and offers career development support. You can read more about these resources here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Katherine Musliner Associate Professor, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Affective Disorders [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4521623283 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 22 Sep 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/associate-professor-of-health-services-research-in-prehospital-emergency-medical-services-ems": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Health Services Research in Prehospital Emergency Medical Services (EMS)",
      "description": "Associate Professor of Health Services Research in Prehospital Emergency Medical Services (EMS) - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Clinical Medicine - Prehospital Emergency Medical Services - Faculty: Health Sciences Associate Professor of Health Services Research in Prehospital Emergency Medical Services (EMS) Deadline 22 Sep 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Dec Department of Clinical Medicine - Prehospital Emergency Medical Services Olof Palmes Allé 34 8200 Aarhus N Part-time position 7.4 hours per week ID: 168580 Apply The Department of Clinical Medicine at Faculty of Health at Aarhus University invites applications for a position as Associate Professor in the field of health services research with a focus on coherence and organisation in the near and prehospital healthcare system as per 1 December 2026. The position is a permanent, 20 pct. part-time position. Department of Clinical Medicine, Aarhus University As an Associate Professor at the Department of Clinical Medicine, you will be part of what is probably the largest health science research department in Denmark. Our clinical research covers all the medical specialties and takes place in close collaboration with the university hospital and the regional hospitals in the Central Denmark Region. We have approx. 30,000 square metres of modern research facilities for experimental surgery and medicine, animal facilities and also advanced scanners at our disposal. The department has overall responsibility for the Master's degree programs in medicine and in molecular medicine. At the department we are approx. 670 academic employees, 500 PhD students and 160 technical/administrative employees who are cooperating across disciplines. As an Associate Professor, you will be working at Aarhus University Hospital or another hospital in the Central Denmark Region. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . Research & Development, Prehospital EMS, Central Denmark Region As an associate professor, you will be based at Research & Development (R&D), Prehospital EMS, Central Denmark Region. R&D is formally affiliated with the Department of Clinical Medicine, Aarhus University, and is anchored in a strong practice-based prehospital research environment. The department works closely with the entire Prehospital EMS organisation, where research, development, clinical practice and implementation are closely integrated. Since 1 January 2026, the University Clinic for Prehospital Care has been integrated into R&D following joint accreditation by Aarhus University and the Central Denmark Region. The University Clinic further strengthens the close integration of research, clinical practice and education within R&D. R&D’s Research Strategy 2026–2030 has the vision Landmark knowledge for the benefit of the patient and is organised around four research themes: Critical Decision Making; Critical Interventions; The Individual and the System; and Coherence and Organisation. The position is part of the new research theme Coherence and Organisation , which explores how coherent patient pathways can be organised across organisational boundaries to support a sustainable healthcare system. The theme draws on perspectives from health services research, implementation science, organisation studies and user involvement. It responds to ongoing changes in the healthcare system, where assessment, treatment and follow-up increasingly take place closer to citizens through collaboration between prehospital services, hospitals, general practice, municipalities and civil society. Your job responsibilities As Associate Professor of Health Services Research in Prehospital Emergency Medical Services your primary tasks will be research and research-based teaching. You will teach and supervise students at bachelor’s and master’s level, and you will contribute to the development of the department through research of high international quality. In your daily work, you will work closely with both Danish and international colleagues. You are expected to establish and lead an internationally recognised research programme within the new research theme Coherence and Organisation . Your main tasks will consist of: Independent research of high international quality, including publication. Teaching, guidance and examination of Bachelor’s and Master's degree students, as well as possibly supervision of PhD students. Some degree of contribution towards attracting external research funding. Establish and lead an internationally recognised research programme within the new research theme Coherence and Organisation. Conduct research on coherent patient pathways across organisational boundaries to support a sustainable healthcare system and develop interdisciplinary and cross-sectoral research collaborations. You will report to the Head of Department Christian Fynbo Christiansen. Your competences You have documented research qualifications within health services research or a related discipline at a high international level. Experience with research in prehospital emergency medical services is expected. You are a clear communicator and have experience with supervision and teaching at bachelor’s and master’s degree level. We draw attention to completed pedagogical courses in supervision or similar, as well as experience with the establishment of PhD courses and external research collaborations. You have experience conducting interdisciplinary and cross-sectoral research and have demonstrated the ability to establish and sustain research collaborations across healthcare sectors. Experience with implementation research, user involvement and qualitative and/or mixed-methods research will be considered an advantage. As a person, you have good interpersonal skills, are inclusive and team-oriented and able to contribute to a good work environment. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. International applicants are expected to learn Danish, and Aarhus University arranges Danish teaching. In order to be assessed as qualified for an Associate Professor position, you must meet these academic criteria. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Head of Department Christian Fynbo Christiansen tel.: (+45) 9350 8854. Your place of work will be the Department of Research & Development, Prehospital EMS,  Brendstrupgårdsvej 7, DK-8200 Aarhus N, Denmark. Terms of employment Appointment as an associate professor requires research and teaching qualifications at the level that can be achieved through the satisfactory completion of a period of appointment as an assistant professor, but may also be obtained in other ways. The applicant is required to have completed a university pedagogical programme or an equivalent programme. Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . The yearly base salary for a fulltime associate professor is DKK 706.233,06 (including a position related supplement, a pre-agreed associate professor supplememt and pension (18.07%)). Additional supplement(s) can be negotiated dependent on experience, special qualifications and performance of special functions. Authorisation supplement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Researchers recruited from abroad are offered a special researcher tax scheme with a lower tax rate. Travel and moving expenses may be covered according to the employee in question and, to a limited extent, the accompanying family. Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial order on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities . Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - associate professor A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios The five publications stated as the most important in the 'Template for applicant - associate professor' must be submitted Research plan, which is part of the 'Template to applicant - associate professor' can be uploaded separately (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the e-recruitment system (optional) We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including assistance with relocation and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information about the International Staff Office and the range of services here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Christian Fynbo Christiansen Head of Department, Department of Clinical Medicine +4593508854 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4521623283 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 22 Sep 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/associate-professor-of-reproductive-epidemiology": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Reproductive Epidemiology",
      "description": "Associate Professor of Reproductive Epidemiology  - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Public Health - Epidemiology, Environment and Biostatistics - Faculty: Health Sciences Associate Professor of Reproductive Epidemiology Deadline 22 Sep 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Nov Department of Public Health - Epidemiology, Environment and Biostatistics Bartholins Allé 2 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term part-time position 1 Nov 2026 - 31 Oct 2029 7.4 hours per week ID: 168625 Apply The Department of Public Health at Faculty of Health Sciences at Aarhus University invites applications for a position as Associate Professor in the field of Reproductive Epidemiology as per 1 November 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. The position is a fixed-term 20% part-time position for 36 months. The right candidate will be affiliated with the BIOSFER project. BIOSFER investigates how social, biological, and psychological factors shape emerging fertility patterns among young adults, and to what extent differences in fertility outcomes between social groups can be explained by social and biomedical factors and is funded by an ERC Synergy Grant. The project brings together researchers from Aarhus University, the Max Planck Institute for Demographic Research (MPIDR), and the Centre for Fertility and Health at the Norwegian Institute of Public Health. About the Department The Department of Public Health is a research-intensive environment dedicated to advancing both fundamental scientific knowledge and applied solutions to major public health challenges. Our research spans theoretical, basic, and applied sciences, with the shared ambition of generating impactful knowledge and improving population health. Academic excellence is at the core of everything we do. We are committed to conducting rigorous and innovative research and delivering high-quality teaching. The department is responsible for teaching within public health, sport science, health science, nursing science, and related methodological disciplines, as well as a substantial part of the teaching in the medical degree programme. While firmly rooted in our respective disciplines, we actively foster interdisciplinary collaboration to advance innovation and excellence in both research and education. We are equally committed to cultivating a healthy, inclusive, and supportive workplace culture characterized by open dialogue, mutual respect, and an informal atmosphere. We believe that academic excellence and personal well-being are mutually reinforcing and essential to long-term success. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . Your job responsibilities As an Associate Professor of Reproductive Epidemiology, you will contribute actively to the department's research and teaching activities. You are expected to conduct research at a high international level and to develop an independent academic profile through both individual scholarship and collaboration with colleagues. You are committed to delivering high-quality teaching and supervision and bring collaborative skills, intellectual curiosity, and a willingness to contribute broadly across the educational programmes and courses for which the department is responsible. You are eager to engage in the academic community at Aarhus University and contribute to strengthening the department's national and international profile. We also value academic citizenship. This includes active participation in collegial collaboration, mentoring, and the social and professional life of the department. We therefore expect you to be an engaged and visible member of the department's daily academic environment. Your primary responsibilities will include: Conducting and publishing research of high international quality, including in leading journals and publishers within your field. Developing your research profile and contributing to the department's strategic objectives, including through applications for external research funding. Teaching and supervising students at Bachelor's and Master's level and contributing to the continued development of innovative teaching activities. Associate Professors are expected to take a leading role and contribute also to the faculty's PhD courses. Disseminating research findings to academic audiences, stakeholders, and the wider public. You will report to the Head of Department Michael Baggesen Klitgaard. Your competences You have established yourself as a recognised researcher within your field and can document research and teaching qualifications at a high international level – pre- and postgraduate. You have a well-developed international network, experience in attracting significant external research funding from national and international funding bodies, and clear potential and motivation to establish yourself as a research leader, establish your own research group, and consolidate an independent research agenda. In the assessment process, particular emphasis will be placed on experience gained through employment, visiting appointments, or extended research stays at recognised universities or research institutions outside the candidate's primary institution. Your personal qualities include the ability to build trust-based relationships with colleagues, students, and external partners. As a teacher, you communicate current and relevant knowledge in a clear, engaging, and inspiring manner. As a supervisor, you are professional, inclusive, and supportive. You value collaboration and contribute positively to the collegial culture that characterises the Department of Public Health, the Faculty of Health Sciences, and Aarhus University more broadly. You are motivated to contribute to the department's continued development and to help maintain an inclusive, supportive, and high-performing work environment. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. International applicants are expected to learn Danish, and Aarhus University arranges Danish teaching. In order to be assessed as qualified for an Associate Professor position, you must meet these academic criteria . Shortlisting will be used. To be considered for the position, applicants must meet the formal requirements for academic appointments at Aarhus University and submit all required application materials by the deadline. Applications that are substantially incomplete will not proceed to further assessment Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Head of Department Michael Baggesen Klitgaard tel.: (+45) 4022 7649 Your place of work will be at the Department of Public Health, Bartholins Allé 2, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. Terms of employment Appointment as an associate professor requires research and teaching qualifications at the level that can be achieved through the satisfactory completion of a period of appointment as an assistant professor, but may also be obtained in other ways. The applicant is required to have completed a university pedagogical programme or an equivalent programme. Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . The yearly base salary for a fulltime associate professor is DKK 706.233,06 (including a position related supplement, a pre-agreed associate professor supplememt and pension (18.07%)). Additional supplement(s) can be negotiated dependent on experience, special qualifications and performance of special functions. Authorisation supplement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Researchers recruited from abroad are offered a special researcher tax scheme with a lower tax rate. Travel and moving expenses may be covered according to the employee in question and, to a limited extent, the accompanying family. Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial order on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities . Application Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - associate professor A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios The five publications stated as the most important in the 'Template for applicant - associate professor' must be submitted Research plan, which is part of the 'Template to applicant - associate professor' can be uploaded separately (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the e-recruitment system (optional) We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including assistance with relocation and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information about the International Staff Office and the range of services here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Michael Baggesen Klitgaard Head of Department, Department of Public Health +4540227649 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4593521835 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 22 Sep 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/student-assistant-for-the-department-of-archaeology-and-heritage-studies": {
      "title": "Student Assistant for the Department of Archaeology and Heritage Studies",
      "description": "Student Assistant for the Department of Archaeology and Heritage Studies - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact School of Culture and Society - Department of Archeology and Heritage Studies Student Assistant for the Department of Archaeology and Heritage Studies Deadline 22 Sep 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Oct School of Culture and Society - Department of Archeology and Heritage Studies Moesgård Allé 20 8270 Højbjerg Part-time position 2.75 hours per week ID: 168611 Apply The Department of Archaeology and Heritage Studies, located at Moesgaard Campus, is recruiting a Student Assistant (studentermedhjælper HK) to support the organisation and communication of our research seminars and related events. Your primary responsibilities will include: Setting up, overseeing, and clearing up receptions following the seminars. The seminars are held on Thursdays at Moesgaard Campus, usually in Foredragssalen, followed by a reception in the staff lounge. Advertising research seminars and related events through social media, posters and other relevant channels, including putting up and removing posters at Moesgaard. Ordering and/or purchasing drinks and snacks for seminar receptions. Assisting with other ad hoc tasks related to the seminars and associated research activities. Qualifications and requirements: We are looking for someone who can work independently, carefully, and reliably, with a strong eye for detail and a commitment to quality. You should be engaged, interested and academically curious, and willing to play an active role in sustaining and developing the research culture at Moesgaard. As the position involves close and regular engagement with research seminars and related academic activities, it may be particularly well suited to a student who is interested in pursuing an academic career in the longer term. You must be enrolled as an active student at a Danish higher education institution. A valid driver’s license is required for the position. As most activities take place at Moesgaard Campus, priority will be given to candidates who are currently enrolled in the bachelor’s or master’s degree programme in Archaeology or Sustainable Heritage Management at Aarhus University. Bachelor’s students are particularly encouraged to apply, as we are looking for someone who will ideally be able to remain in the position for a longer period of time. We would like the successful candidate to start on 1 October 2026 , or as soon as possible thereafter. The position is expected to involve approximately 2–3 hours of work per week during the semesters, until the completion of their studies. How to apply: Please upload your application in English or Danish (maximum one A4 page) and your CV/ resume. Clearly state your current stage of study, as employment in this position is conditional upon your student status. If you have any questions about the position, you are welcome to contact [email protected] Løn og ansættelsesvilkår Du ansættes efter fællesoverenskomsten mellem Finansministeriet og HK. Aarhus Universitet vil være en attraktiv og inspirerende arbejdsplads for alle og ønsker en kultur, hvor hver enkelt kan udfolde og udvikle sig. Vi ser ligestilling og diversitet som en ressource og opfordrer derfor alle interesserede til at ansøge. Om Arts Faculty of Arts er et af Nordeuropas væsentligste fakulteter for forskning og videregående uddannelse inden for humaniora, teologi og pædagogik. Inden for denne faglige bredde huser fakultetet omkring 700 forskere, 200 ph.d.-studerende, 9.000 bachelor- og kandidatstuderende og 1.500 efter- og videreuddannelsesstuderende. Vi tror på, at de bedste idéer opstår i mødet mellem forskellige perspektiver – og at forskning, undervisning og innovation udvikles bedst i dialog med samfundet omkring os. Du kan finde mere information her Faculty of Arts | Aarhus Universitet Ansøgning sendes via Aarhus Universitets rekrutteringssystem, som kan tilgås under stillingsopslaget på Aarhus Universitets hjemmeside. Questions about the position\n                            ? Cecilie Wilsborg PhD Student, School of Culture and Society - Department of Archeology and Heritage Studies +4587151391 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Arts Administrative Centre - Arts HR +4593521049 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 22 Sep 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
