    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoctoral-researcher-in-single-crystal-x-ray-diffraction-materials-and-chemical-science": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Single-Crystal X-ray Diffraction (Materials and Chemical Science)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Single-Crystal X-ray Diffraction (Materials and Chemical Science) - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Chemistry Postdoctoral Researcher in Single-Crystal X-ray Diffraction (Materials and Chemical Science) Deadline 2 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Department of Chemistry Langelandsgade 140 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 168444 Apply We are seeking a highly motivated candidate with expertise in single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) for a two-year postdoctoral position in materials science at the DanMAX and  SINCRYS beamlines at MAX IV Laboratory in Lund, Sweden. SINCRYS is currently under construction and will soon enter its commissioning phase. The beamline is expected to receive first light late 2026, with expert commissioning commencing in 2027. The candidate will participate in the commissioning, testing, and optimization of the beamline. SINCRYS is built for SCXRD studies of small-unit-cell systems commonly encountered in organic molecules, inorganic materials, and hybrid organic-inorganic compounds. While its primary focus is on high-throughput structure determination of micro-crystals, the beamline has been designed to also support a range of advanced crystallographic experiments. As a result, planning, preparing, conducting, and supporting user experiments will be an integral part of the position. SINCRYS will serve as the foundation of the Scandinavian Crystallography Service (SCS), a one-stop shop for mail-in and remote-access crystallography. The SCS will serve a broad user community, including academic researchers and industrial partners, particularly those with limited prior experience of synchrotron-based research or single-crystal diffraction techniques. The successful candidate will play a pivotal role as the principal SCS crystallographer. Key responsibilities will include outreach, SCXRD data collection, data processing, structure solution, and user support. To make SCS a success, the successful candidate is expected to engage in outreach and community activities, including travel within Scandinavia to promote the service, build collaborations, and provide guidance and training to users. In addition to supporting user operations and SCS, the successful candidate will have the opportunity to pursue a research program under the supervision of the SINCRYS beamline scientist. The commissioning of a new synchrotron beamline provides a unique environment for developing novel diffraction methodologies, data-analysis workflows, and experimental capabilities. The candidate will have access to state-of-the-art synchrotron instrumentation and be encouraged to initiate and participate in collaborative research projects in structural chemistry and materials science, working closely with researchers in the DanMAX/SINCRYS team, Aarhus University and MAX IV. Depending on the candidate’s interests and expertise, research activities may include studies of functional materials, advanced crystallographic methods, automated and high-throughput crystallography, or the development of new applications enabled by the beamline. The combination of beamline commissioning, crystallography service, and research provides an excellent platform for developing a long-term career in synchrotron science and/or crystallography. The SINCRYS project is led by Aarhus University and carried out in close collaboration with MAX IV Laboratory, Sweden's national synchrotron facility hosted by Lund University. The successful candidate will be employed and managed by Aarhus University, Denmark, while the primary workplace will be at MAX IV Laboratory in Lund, Sweden. Key Responsibilities The SCS Crystallographer will, in collaboration with the SINCRYS beamline scientist: Participate in the commissioning, testing, and optimization of the SINCRYS beamline and its experimental workflows Promote the SCS through outreach activities and engagement with academic and industrial partners across Scandinavia Collaborate with beamline scientists, software developers, and technical staff to improve instrumentation and user workflows Collect, process, and interpret SCXRD data for academic and industrial users Perform synchrotron-based single-crystal X-ray diffraction experiments on a wide range of materials Program and establish analytic tools to assess data quality Support users in experiment planning, sample preparation, data analysis, and structure determination Contribute to the development of mail-in and remote-access crystallography services Opportunities and career development The successful candidate will: Be part of the commissioning and scientific development of a new synchrotron beamline from first light onward Access state-of-the-art synchrotron X-ray instrumentation at MAX IV, one of the world's leading synchrotron facilities Publish scientific results in scientific journals and present research at international conferences Gain experience in large-scale research infrastructure and beamline operations Play a leading role in shaping the Scandinavian Crystallography Service and expanding its scientific reach Build a strong professional network within the international synchrotron and crystallography communities. Qualification requirements Applicants must have: A PhD in a relevant discipline (e.g., crystallography, chemistry, materials science, physics) Experience in chemical/materials crystallography (e.g. small unit-cell systems) Expert knowledge of single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) and in SCXRD data processing Very good oral and written proficiency in English Assessment criteria and other qualifications: Experience in the Python programming language Experience in mounting micro-crystals Experience with experiments at synchrotron radiation sources Consideration will also be given to good collaborative skills, drive, independence, and how the applicant’s experience and skills complement and strengthen ongoing research within the team. Terms of employment Two-year position with preferred start date 1 January 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. The place of work is the MAX IV Laboratory in Lund, Fotongatan 2, Sweden, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with affiliated institutions. Contact information Contact person: Lennard Krause, +4571604837, [email protected] Deadline All applications must be received no later than 2 October 2026. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the appointment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. All applicants will be notified whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee, and each applicant is given the opportunity to comment on the part of the assessment that concerns him/her self. Formalities and salary range Natural Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation . The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . Aarhus University also offers a Junior Researcher Development Programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here . At the Faculty of Natural Science at Aarhus University, we strive to support our scientific staff in their career development. We focus on competency development and career clarification and want to make your opportunities transparent. On our website , you can find information on all types of scientific positions, as well as the entry criteria we use when assessing candidates. You can also read more about how we can assist you in your career planning and development. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Lennard Krause Researcher (Tenure Track), Department of Chemistry +4571604837 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522228426 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Friday  2 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
