      "title": "Associate Professor in ‘Sensory and Consumer Innovation’",
      "description": "Associate Professor in ‘Sensory and Consumer Innovation’ - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Food Science - Food Quality Perception & Society - Faculty: Technical Sciences Associate Professor in ‘Sensory and Consumer Innovation’ Deadline 5 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Dec Department of Food Science - Food Quality Perception & Society Agro Food Park 48 8200 Aarhus N Full-time position ID: 168509 Apply The Department of Food Science, Aarhus University invites applications for an Associate Professor position in Sensory and Consumer Science to begin by December 1st 2026 or as soon as possible thereafter. The position This Associate Professor position will establish and strengthen a strategic research profile in Sensory and Consumer Innovation at the Department of Food Science. The position will build on the Department’s internationally recognised expertise in sensory and consumer science while extending its application towards food innovation, product development, consumer-driven design and value creation. With a particular focus on the development of future foods, products and experiences, the position will investigate how human sensory perception, consumer behaviour and preferences can be translated into innovative solutions for high-quality, healthy and sustainable food and beverage applications, with potential for value creation and market uptake. The position will contribute to the development of new approaches that connect sensory and consumer insights with food innovation, product development and commercialisation. A key ambition is to strengthen the translation of sensory and consumer capabilities into innovation opportunities in collaboration with industry and other stakeholders, including the development and evaluation of new food products, concepts, experiences and consumer-oriented solutions. The position will also further develop the Department’s capacity to combine sensory science, consumer science, food quality, innovation and market-oriented research. The successful candidate will contribute to interdisciplinary research across food science, nutrition, health, sustainability and innovation, and strengthen collaboration with industry and other relevant stakeholders. The position is aligned with the Science Team ‘Food Quality Perception and Society’ (FQS) at the Department of Food Science http://food.au.dk/en/foodresearch/science-teams/food-quality-perception-society/. The Science Team provides access to state-of-the-art sensory and analytical facilities, including a professional trained and tested sensory panel and an ISO-approved sensory laboratory. The team combines human sensory and consumer measurements with advanced instrumental techniques and in-house analytical infrastructure, including e.g. GC-MS, LC-MS and NMR. A central ambition of the position is to develop sensory and consumer science as an innovation capability, creating new methods, services and approaches that can be applied in collaboration with food companies, technology providers, public organisations and other stakeholders. This includes identifying consumer needs and opportunities, supporting concept and product development, evaluating prototypes and new food experiences, and developing approaches for translating consumer insight into successful innovation and market applications. The successful candidate is expected to develop an internationally recognised research profile while contributing to teaching, supervision, external funding and strategic collaborations. Particular emphasis will be placed on building partnerships with industry and other stakeholders and translating sensory and consumer knowledge into new products, services and commercial opportunities, thereby strengthening the role of sensory and consumer science as a driver of food innovation, helping to position the Department as a leading partner for consumer-driven food innovation. The candidate Qualifications and expectations The successful candidate must have a strong and internationally recognised research profile in sensory and consumer science, with expertise in applying sensory and consumer insights to food quality, innovation and the development of new food products, concepts and experiences. The candidate should demonstrate the ability to connect fundamental sensory and consumer research with consumer-driven food innovation, product development and value creation, particularly in the context of healthier, more sustainable and high-quality foods. The candidate must demonstrate outstanding potential for independent research, dissemination and successful acquisition of competitive external funding. The candidate should demonstrate a clear ability to develop novel and independent research initiatives and to establish an internationally visible research programme. Experience with international research collaboration and a strong record of international peer-reviewed publications will be expected. The successful candidate will be expected to: •    deliver innovative research of the highest international standard in sensory and consumer innovation; •    establish and develop a research area within the existing FQS Science Team; •    develop new approaches for translating sensory and consumer knowledge into food innovation, product development and value creation; •    publish research outcomes through a significant number of high-quality international peer-reviewed publications; •    develop and strengthen collaborations with industry and other relevant stakeholders; •    contribute to the development of new sensory and consumer methods, capabilities and applications with potential for innovation and market uptake; •    contribute to teaching at the Department, including graduate-level teaching in English; •    supervise Master's and PhD students; •    complete postgraduate teacher training for Assistant Professors at Aarhus University; •    attract competitive research funding from national and international external funding bodies; and •    communicate fluently in English, both orally and in writing. Qualifications •    a relevant PhD and a minimum of 2–3 years of postdoctoral research experience in Food Science, Sensory Science, Consumer Science, Food Innovation or an equivalent field; •    strong competencies in sensory and consumer analysis, including the application of sensory and consumer methods to food quality, product development and innovation; •    experience in consumer-driven food innovation, including understanding consumer needs, preferences and behaviour and translating consumer insights into new food products, concepts or experiences; •    experience with univariate and multivariate statistical analysis of sensory and consumer data, including the ability to integrate sensory and consumer data with instrumental and other multidisciplinary datasets; •    experience in developing or applying innovative sensory and consumer methodologies will be an advantage; •    experience of working with industry and other external stakeholders, particularly in relation to food innovation, product development, knowledge exchange or value creation, will be an advantage; •    experience with cross-cultural consumer research and/or different food cultures and contexts will be an advantage; •    a proven publication record in international peer-reviewed journals, with a significant number of high-quality publications appropriate to career stage; •    experience in teaching and supervision at MSc and PhD level; and •    demonstrated potential to develop an independent research programme and attract competitive external research funding. •    experience in international research collaboration and networking The Department of Food Science The Department of Food Science is part of the Faculty of Science and Technology, Aarhus University and includes research within the areas Sensory and Consumer Science, Biofunctional Food, Systems Biology, Food Chemistry and Technology, Food Quality, Food Metabolomics and Plant Production. The Department currently employs 60 full time scientific staff, approx. 50 PhD students and Postdocs, plus approx. 30 visiting students and staff who together contribute to an international research environment where English is the preferred language in the laboratory, at meetings and at seminars. The Department works in teams and therefore self-leadership, clear and open communication, success in mentoring, supervising and teaching are important qualities needed to be successful in the Department. In addition the Department has a strong culture that is based on openness, equality, consultation, inclusion and contributing to the Department strategy and working environment are viewed as important. The Department is responsible for a Master’s programme in Molecular Nutrition and Food Technology, taught at Aarhus University, Denmark, and a coming Master’s programme in International Food Quality and Health at the Sino-Danish Center in Beijing, China. In addition, Department staff contribute to several other key interdisciplinary educations, including Bachelor’s and Master’s programmes in Agrobiology, and a Bachelor of Chemical Engineering in Food Technology at Aarhus University. Staff also teach on a number of other educations offered at other Universities in Denmark and China. Please refer to http://food.au.dk/en/ for further information about The Department of Food Science and to https://tech.au.dk/en/ and http://www.au.dk/en/ for information on Technical Sciences and Aarhus University, respectively. Employment and place of work The employer is Aarhus University. The place of employment is Aarhus University and the place of work is at the Food Quality Perception and Society Science Team at Department of Food Science (http://food.au.dk/en/ ), Agro Food Park 48, 8200 Aarhus, Denmark. The Department of Food Science offers: • a well-developed research infrastructure, laboratories and access to shared equipment • an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators • a research climate encouraging lively, open and critical discussion within and across different fields of research • a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities • a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance. For further information about the position, please contact Prof. Derek V. Byrne at [email protected] . Living in Denmark Please visit the International Staff Office website ( https://internationalstaff.au.dk/ ) for information about arriving and living in Denmark. Technical Sciences Tenure Track Aarhus University offers talented scientists from around the world attractive career perspectives via the Technical Sciences Tenure Track Programme. Highly qualified candidates are appointed as Assistant Professors for a period of six years with the prospect of performance- based advancement to a tenured Associate Professorship. The aim of the Technical Sciences Tenure Track Programme is to: attract outstanding talented individuals that are competitive at an international level to promote the early development of independent research success early in the career of scientists to create transparency in the academic career path As part of the tenure track position, the candidate is offered: access to research infrastructure capability development, including postgraduate teacher training a mentoring programme support to develop scientific networks and to secure interdisciplinary research at the highest level As part of the Aarhus University Tenure Track Programme, the University carries out a mid-way evaluation to review the progress of the tenure track candidate after three years, according to the same criteria used in the final tenure review. The final tenure review is conducted after five and a half years. If the review is positive, the candidate will be offered a tenured position as Associate Professor at Aarhus University. Please refer to the tenure track guidelines for the tenure review criteria and for the tenure review process. Application procedure Shortlisting is used. This means that after the deadline for applications – and with the assistance from the assessment committee chairman, and the assessment committee if necessary, – the head of department selects the candidates to be evaluated. The selection is made on the basis of an assessment of who of the candidates are most relevant considering the requirements of the advertisement. All applicants will be notified within 6 weeks whether or not their applications have been sent to an expert assessment committee for evaluation. The selected applicants will be informed about the composition of the committee and will receive his/her assessment. Formalities and salary range Technical Sciences refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities under the Danish Ministry of Science, Technology and Innovation. The application must be in English and include a curriculum vitae, degree certificate, a complete list of publications, a statement of future research plans and information about research activities, teaching portfolio and verified information on previous teaching experience (if any). Guidelines for applicants can be found here. Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions at basic salary steps 4-8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Research activities will be evaluated in relation to actual research time. Thus, we encourage applicants to specify periods of leave without research activities, in order to be able to subtract these periods from the span of the scientific career during the evaluation of scientific productivity. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including relocation service and career counselling to expat partners. Read more here . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website . Questions about the position\n                            ? Derek V. Byrne Professor, Science Team Leader, Department of Food Science - Food Quality Perception & Society +4528782840 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4520941413 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday  5 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/research-assistant-fixed-term-contract-in-the-research-projects-headed-by-professor-rubina-raja-school-of-culture-and-society-aarhus-university": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Research assistant (fixed-term contract) in the research projects headed by Professor Rubina Raja, School of Culture and Society, Aarhus University - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact School of Culture and Society - Unit for Eastern Mediterranean Archaeology, History and Legacy Data - Faculty: Arts Research assistant (fixed-term contract) in the research projects headed by Professor Rubina Raja, School of Culture and Society, Aarhus University Deadline 5 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan School of Culture and Society - Unit for Eastern Mediterranean Archaeology, History and Legacy Data Jens Chr. Skous Vej 5 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term part-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 30 Sep 2027 25 hours per week ID: 169016 Apply The School of Culture and Society, Faculty of Arts, Aarhus University (AU), we are looking to recruit a fixed-term research assistant (25 hour per week) for the period 1 January 2027 to 30 September 2027. Place of employment: Department of History and Classical Studies, Institute for Culture and Society, Aarhus University, Jens Chr. Skous Vej 5, 8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. The position The successful candidate is expected to work closely with Professor Rubina Raja, on several tasks, including: supporting large research/publication projects (including copyediting and indexing) carrying out data collection, data management and analysis (under supervision) performing research tasks within the area of classical archaeology, e.g. literature searches and image processing supporting processes in connection with funding applications/reporting to funding agencies. Qualifications Applicants are expected to have the following qualifications: an MA in classical archaeology Proven experience in archaeology and cultural heritage of the Eastern Mediterranean, Near East and Western Asia fluency in English (spoken and written) knowledge of other modern languages such as French, German and Italian excellent attention to detail, including in terms of copyediting, proofreading, and information gathering, including source-critical approache experience with editorial work and copyediting flexibility and the ability of make shifts in focus depending on amounts and importance of assignments and deadlines a methodological and structured approach to work, and exceptional organisational skills excellent time-management skills and ability to meet deadlines, including in relation to publication projects excellent interpersonal and collaborative skills, including in terms of coordinating complex data collection processes and publication processes with other team members. Applicants who can document experience from similar roles will have an advantage. Further information For further information about the position, please contact Professor Rubina Raja ( [email protected] ) For more information about applications, please contact HR ( [email protected] ). About the project The LoCiS project investigates the rich but understudied local portrait cultures of ancient Western Asia with the aim of determining how they reflect local and regional entanglements with and responses to different imperial hegemonies. LoCiS aims to reveal new knowledge about intersecting identities on an individual, local and regional level through a long durée perspective with a global historical outlook. The thousands of surviving portraits have not been studied comprehensively – neither diachronically nor in contexts or in cross-regional perspectives. LoCiS aims at offering unprecedented insights into the multilayered narratives of the varied individual experiences in antiquity. Formalities Faculty of Arts refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities (the Appointment Order) . Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Finance and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Further information on the application and supplementary materials may be found in Application Guidelines . Shortlists may be prepared with the candidates that have been selected for a detailed academic assessment. A committee set up by the head of school is responsible for selecting the most qualified candidates. See this link for further information about shortlisting at the Faculty of Arts: shortlisting Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Terms of employment Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement be-tween the Danish Ministry of Finance and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Salary and terms of employment are governed by the Cirkulære om overenskomst for akademikere i staten (in Danish). You will be employed as (xxx) at base pay level 4–8 with additional supplements in accord-ance with the Cirkulære om protokollat om visse ansættelsesvilkår for videnskabeligt personale ved universite-ter (in Danish). The job content and qualification requirements are described in further detail in the Ministerial Order on the Appoint-ment of  Academic Staff at Universities . About Arts The Faculty of Arts is one of Northern Europe’s most significant faculties for research and higher education in the humanities, theology and education. Within this broad academic scope, the faculty comprises approximately 700 researchers, 200 PhD students, 9,000 Bachelor’s and Master’s degree students, and 1,500 further and continuing education students. We believe that the best ideas arise when different perspectives meet – and that research, teaching and innovation are best developed in dialogue with the surrounding society. You can find more information here: Faculty of Arts | Aarhus University The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Rubina Raja Professor, School of Culture and Society - Classical Archaeology, subject +4527188390 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Arts Administrative Centre - Arts HR +4593521049 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday  5 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoctoral-position-at-the-department-of-anthropology-aarhus-university": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral position",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position at the Department of Anthropology, Aarhus University - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology - Faculty: Arts Postdoctoral position at the Department of Anthropology, Aarhus University Deadline 6 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology Moesgård Allé 20 8270 Højbjerg Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 168656 Apply The Department of Anthropology at the School of Culture and Society, Faculty of Arts, Aarhus University invites applications for a full time (37h/w) 2-year fixed-term postdoctoral position in the Danmarks Frie Forskningsfond / Independent Research Fund Denmark (DFF) research project “Beyond the Hoof: How Cattle Production shaped landscapes and anthropological knowledge of the Kalahari Desert.” The appointment begins on January 1, 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The place of work is Aarhus University, Moesgaard Allé 20, 8270 Højbjerg. The university is keen for its staff to reflect the diversity of society and thus welcomes applications from all qualified applicants regardless of their personal background . Research context The “Beyond the Hoof” project seeks an innovative postdoctoral scholar in anthropology to lead a research package about the social and ecological impacts of cattle and cattle infrastructures in the Kalahari Desert in Botswana, Africa. The position is part of the Sapere-Aude: DFF Research Leaders project, led by associate professor Pierre du Plessis, that investigates how Botswana’s commercial cattle industry has reshaped landscapes beyond the immediate sites of production, and how the changes they induced influenced anthropological study of the Kalahari. Focusing on infrastructures such as veterinary fences and boreholes, the study traces ripple effects “beyond the hoof”—fragmented wildlife migrations, altered vegetation, and disrupted San hunter-gatherer territories—that not only shaped and continue to shape Kalahari landscapes, but also the very conditions of anthropological observation and theory about human-environmental relations. The candidate will be responsible for a work package that investigates contemporary processes of cattle expansion in an historical hunting and gathering territory, the implications for both cultural and natural heritage in this area, and how communities experience and perceive these transformations. The position thus involves significant periods of field research between cattle-grazing and wildlife management areas, conducting interviews, community-based mapping activities, gathering oral histories, and participation in ecological surveys. The ideal candidate will have an existing research profile that uses ethnographic methods to explore questions relevant to the social and ecological effects of livestock and agricultural infrastructures on landscapes as well as a keen interest in the history of anthropology (particularly as it relates to the Kalahari and southern African). The postdoctoral fellow will be expected to contribute to the theoretical and methodological analysis of how landscape change influenced the historical development of environmental anthropology in the Kalahari and should be prepared to work in a collaborative research team with the PI and project affiliates, including interdisciplinary research bridging social and natural science. The successful applicant will be expected to: Both assist in ongoing research and conduct original ethnographic field research on the intersection of cattle and human-wildlife interactions, infrastructural expansion, and cultural and natural heritage of Kalahari landscapes in Botswana. Develop interdisciplinary collaborative research with the PI and project affiliates. Conduct literature reviews about Kalahari Anthropology Publish sole-author and collaborative publications based on project research findings. Present findings at conferences, workshops, lectures, and stakeholder meetings. Participate in the theoretical and methodological development of the larger “Beyond the Hoof” project with the PI. Assist in coordinating and organizing project activities and outputs. Coordinate field research with the PI to be conducted annually in Botswana. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct 3-4 months of field research in Botswana in both 2027 and 2028 (this will occur concurrently with the PI’s field research periods). The successful applicant will be required to reside in Denmark and maintain residence in the country (including during periods of field research). Qualifications : PhD in Anthropology or equal qualifications (environmental humanities, geography, political ecology, archaeology) Should have immersive ethnographic field research experience, with preference given to research experience in southern Africa. Familiarity with the anthropological landscape of Botswana, and the Kalahari in particular, is beneficial. Fluency in English; additional familiarity with regional languages is advantageous. Must have demonstrated experience conducting original ethnographic research on: Landscapes, more-than-human relations, infrastructure, agricultural development, and/or human-wildlife conflict, including impacts on ecosystems and livelihoods. Hunter-gatherer and/or pastoral economies and ecologies. Foraging and/or herding based livelihood strategies and their relations to savannah ecosystems. Indigenous land use, including both subsistence and market production. Local institutions of property / territorial access. Using historical and archival sources to understand landscape and land use changes across time. Analysis of historical ethnography Expertise in rural livelihood strategies, including hunting, gathering, and/or herding, agroecology and agrarian studies, multispecies ethnography and landscape analysis, political ecology, cultural heritage, African studies, southern African studies, Indigenous studies, political economy, territorial conflicts in the context of biodiversity conservation. Familiarity with existing and historical research efforts in the Kalahari (i.e. Harvard Kalahari Research Group, Kyoto School, etc.) is not required but highly beneficial. Experience conducting and coordinating interdisciplinary research between the natural and social sciences is advantageous. Teaching The position will involve minimal teaching as agreed upon with the Head of the Department and the project PI. Knowledge exchange The successful applicant will be expected to exchange knowledge with various sectors of society and to contribute actively to public debate in areas related to the position. To apply please submit: Cover letter outlining qualifications, describing existing research, and describing your interest in and motivation for participating in the project (maximum 2 pages). Current CV (including a list publications) A maximum of 2 publications (Only submitted publications will be assessed; a list of publications is not sufficient. As a result, applications without submitted publications will not be assessed.) The application must be submitted in English. The research activities will be evaluated in relation to the actual research time. We therefore encourage applicants to specify any periods of leave they may have had without research activities (e.g. maternity/paternity leave), so we can subtract these periods from the span of their academic career when evaluating their productivity . Only submitted publications will be assessed; a list of publications is not sufficient. As a result, applications without submitted publications will not be assessed. Professional references or recommendations should not be included. Applicants who are selected for an interview may be asked to provide professional references. For further information about the position, please contact [email protected] For further information about the application procedure, please contact HR  ( [email protected] ) International applicants International applicants are encouraged to check Family and work-life balance and Attractive working conditions for further information about the benefits of working at Aarhus University and in Denmark, including healthcare, paid holidays and, if relevant, maternity/paternity leave, childcare and schooling. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including a relocation service and career counselling for expat partners. For information about taxation, see Taxation aspects of international researchers’ employment by AU . The work environment The School of Culture and Society The School of Culture and Society is a vibrant research community dedicated to understanding the cultural and social conditions that shape human life. Our work examines how people interact with their environments, from prehistory to the present, and across both local contexts and global dynamics. Through cross-sector and cross-disciplinary partnerships, we aim to drive research innovation and contribute to address the major questions facing society. In both research and education, we are committed to bringing the humanities into active dialogue with partners beyond the university. The School is committed to: Fostering an international environment characterized by strong academic and interdisciplinary collaboration. Embracing an innovative and experimental approach to research, teaching, and communication. Cultivating a school that both generates knowledge and educates students for the present and future. Advancing a broad engagement with digital humanities. Upholding a strong commitment to both local and global societal challenges. Creating an inclusive, diverse, and sustainable workplace and organization. Read more about the school here School of Culture and Society - Aarhus University Qualification requirements Applicants should hold a PhD or equivalent academic qualifications. Formalities Faculty of Arts refers to the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Danish Universities (the Appointment Order) . Further information on qualification requirements and job content may be found in the Memorandum on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Danish Universities . Further information on the application and supplementary materials may be found in Application Guidelines . The application must outline the applicant's motivation for applying for the position, attaching a curriculum vitae, a teaching portfolio, a complete list of published works, copies of degree certificates and examples of academic production ( mandatory, but no more than two examples ). Please upload this material electronically along with your application. Aarhus University also offers a junior researcher development programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here: https://talent.au.dk/junior-researcher-development-programme/ If nothing else is noted, applications must be submitted in English. The application deadline is at 11.59 pm Danish time (same as Central European Time) on the deadline day. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. Shortlists may be prepared with the candidates that have been selected for a detailed academic assessment. A committee set up by the head of school is responsible for selecting the most qualified candidates. See this link for further information about shortlisting at the Faculty of Arts: shortlisting Terms of employment Appointment shall be in accordance with the collective labour agreement between the Danish Ministry of Finance and the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations. Salary and terms of employment are governed by the Cirkulære om overenskomst for akademikere i staten (in Danish). You will be employed as a postdoctoral fellow at base pay level 4–8 with additional supplements in accordance with the Cirkulære om protokollat om visse ansættelsesvilkår for videnskabeligt personale ved universiteter (in Danish). The job content and qualification requirements are described in further detail in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of  Academic Staff at Universities . About Arts The Faculty of Arts is one of Northern Europe’s most significant faculties for research and higher education in the humanities, theology and education. Within this broad academic scope, the faculty comprises approximately 700 researchers, 200 PhD students, 9,000 Bachelor’s and Master’s degree students, and 1,500 further and continuing education students. We believe that the best ideas arise when different perspectives meet – and that research, teaching and innovation are best developed in dialogue with the surrounding society. You can find more information here: Faculty of Arts | Aarhus University The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website . Questions about the position\n                            ? Pierre du Plessis Associate Professor, School of Culture and Society - Department of Anthropology Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Arts Administrative Centre - Arts HR +4593521049 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday  6 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/associate-professor-assistant-professor-in-statistical-genetics-or-health-data-science-at-the-pioneer-centre-for-smartbiomed": {
      "title": "Associate Professor / Assistant Professor in statistical genetics or health data science",
      "description": "Associate Professor / Assistant Professor in statistical genetics or health data science at the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Public Health - National Centre for Register-based Research - Faculty: Health Sciences Associate Professor / Assistant Professor in statistical genetics or health data science at the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed Deadline 11 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Department of Public Health - National Centre for Register-based Research Bartholins Allé 2 8000 Aarhus C Full-time position ID: 168738 Apply The Department of Public Health at Faculty of Health Sciences at Aarhus University invites applications for a full-time position as Associate or Assistant Professor at the Pioneer Centre for Statistical and computational Methods for Advanced Research to Transform biomedicine (SMARTbiomed) within the field of statistical and machine learning methods development for genetic analysis and causal inference with a focus on developing and applying statistical, machine learning methods, and AI to analyse population-scale health data and omics data. The position has a start date as of 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The appointment as Associate Professor is permanent, whereas appointment as Assistant Professor is a four-year fixed-term position. About the Department of Public Health The Department of Public Health is a research-intensive environment dedicated to advancing both fundamental scientific knowledge and applied solutions to major public health challenges. Our research spans theoretical, basic, and applied sciences, with the shared ambition of generating impactful knowledge and improving population health. Academic excellence is at the core of everything we do. We are committed to conducting rigorous and innovative research and delivering high-quality teaching. The department is responsible for teaching within public health, sport science, health science, nursing science, and related methodological disciplines, as well as a substantial part of the teaching in the medical degree programme. While firmly rooted in our respective disciplines, we actively foster interdisciplinary collaboration to advance innovation and excellence in both research and education. We are equally committed to cultivating a healthy, inclusive, and supportive workplace culture characterized by open dialogue, mutual respect, and an informal atmosphere. We believe that academic excellence and personal well-being are mutually reinforcing and essential to long-term success. You can read more about the department here and about the faculty here . Your job responsibilities As an Associate or Assistant Professor in statistical genetics or health data science at the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed, you will contribute to the Centre’s research, teaching, and work environment. You are expected to conduct research at a high international level and to develop an independent academic profile through both individual scholarship and collaboration with colleagues. You are committed to delivering high-quality teaching and supervision and bring collaborative skills, intellectual curiosity, and a willingness to contribute broadly across the educational programmes. You are eager to engage in the academic community at Aarhus University and within SMARTbiomed and to contribute to strengthening the department's national and international profile. We also value academic citizenship. This includes active participation in collegial collaboration, mentoring, and the social and professional life of both the department and the centre. We therefore expect you to be an engaged and visible member of the department's daily academic environment. Your primary responsibilities will include: Conducting and publishing research of high international quality, including in leading journals and publishers within your field. Associate Professors are expected to contribute to and lead one or more major research projects focused on deciphering the causal relationship between psychiatric disorders and risk factors Developing your research profile and contributing to the department's strategic objectives, including through applications for external research funding. Teaching at PhD courses and workshops organized by the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed. Assistant professors are expected to participate in the supervision of PhD students, and Associate Professors are expected to take a leading role and contribute also to the faculty's PhD courses. Disseminating research findings to academic audiences, stakeholders, and the wider public. You will be part of the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed and work closely with researchers at our three hub sites. SMARTbiomed is an international collaboration between Aarhus University, University of Oxford, and University of Copenhagen that aims to advance the development of methods and software for analysis and inference from massive human data, advancing applications in medicine. SMARTbiomed is anchored at the National Centre for Register-based Research (NCRR), Department of Public Health at Aarhus University, and led by Professor Naomi Wray. The position is based at our site in Aarhus, but you can expect some travel to our sites in Oxford and Copenhagen. You can read more about SMARTbiomed here and about NCRR here . For the Associate Professor position, we expect you to be an aspiring group leader. Depending on your level an attractive start package can be negotiated, which may include funding for up to one PhD student and one postdoc. You will report to the Head of the National Centre for Register-based Research, Bjarni Vilhjálmsson. Your competences Applicants for Assistant Professorships You hold a PhD degree in a relevant discipline and have demonstrated the potential to become an internationally recognised researcher within statistical genetics, health data sciences, or related research areas. You can document research and teaching experience and show clear promise in developing an independent research agenda. Experience with international research collaboration and applications for external funding will be considered an advantage. You are expected to demonstrate a clear motivation for establishing yourself as a future research leader and for pursuing career-promoting research grants. Although not a requirement, the assessment process will place emphasis on experience gained through employment or extended research stays at recognised universities or research institutions outside the PhD awarding institution. Applicants for Associate Professorships You have established yourself as a recognised researcher within statistical genetics, health data sciences, or related research areas and can document research and teaching qualifications at a high international level – pre- and postgraduate. You have a well-developed international network, experience in attracting significant external research funding from national and international funding bodies, and clear potential and motivation to establish yourself as a research leader with your own research group and consolidate an independent research agenda. In the assessment process, particular emphasis will be placed on experience gained through employment, visiting appointments, or extended research stays at recognised universities or research institutions outside the candidate's primary institution. For both positions, we expect you to have several of the following qualifications: Background in AI, bioinformatics, statistics, computer science, data science, machine learning, epidemiology, or related fields with a relevant PhD degree (required). Experience with analyzing large genetic data sets, e.g., UK Biobank, All-of-Us, or similar data. Experience with developing statistical and computational methods for analyzing genetic or health data. Experience with supervision of PhD students or postdocs. Experience with writing grant applications for external funding. Knowledge of machine learning and epidemiological methods including causal inference and risk prediction. Your personal qualities include the ability to build trust-based relationships with colleagues, students, and external partners. As a teacher, you communicate current and relevant knowledge in a clear, engaging, and inspiring manner. As a supervisor, you are professional, inclusive, and supportive. You value collaboration and contribute positively to the collegial culture that characterises the Department of Public Health, the Faculty of Health Sciences, SMARTbiomed, and Aarhus University more broadly. You are motivated to contribute to the centre´s and the department´s continued development and to help maintain an inclusive, supportive, and high-performing work environment. We expect you to be fluent in oral and written English. International applicants are expected to learn Danish, and Aarhus University arranges Danish teaching. In order to be assessed as qualified for an Associate Professor position, you must meet these academic criteria . In order to be assessed as qualified for an Assistant Professor position, you must meet these academic criteria. Shortlisting will be used. Questions about the position If you have any questions about the position, please contact Head of the National Centre for Register-based Research, Bjarni Vilhjálmsson (email: [email protected] , Phone: +45 24201279) or scientific coordinator at the Pioneer Centre for SMARTbiomed, Gitte Bundgaard Christiansen (email: [email protected] , Phone: +45 20826431). Your place of work will be the National Centre for Register-based Research, Department of Public Health, Bartholins Allé 2, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark. We expect to conduct interviews end of November – beginning of December 2026. Terms of employment – Assistant professor Appointment as an associate professor requires research and teaching qualifications at the level that can be achieved through the satisfactory completion of a period of appointment as an assistant professor, but may also be obtained in other ways. The applicant is required to have completed a university pedagogical programme or an equivalent programme. The yearly base salary for a fulltime associate professor is DKK 706.233,06 (including a position related supplement, a pre-agreed associate professor supplememt and pension (18.07%)). Additional supplement(s) can be negotiated dependent on experience, special qualifications and performance of special functions. Authorisation supplement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Travel and moving expenses may be covered according to the employee in question and, to a limited extent, the accompanying family. Terms of employment – Assistant professor Appointment as an assistant professor requires academic qualifications at PhD level. The yearly base salary for a fulltime assistant professor is between DKK 493.2394,35 and DKK 585.322,48 depending on the years of working experience after achieved MSc degree. The base salary includes a position related supplement and pension (18.07 %). Additional supplement(s) for special qualifications can be negotiated. Authorisation supplement(s) will be granted, if relevant for the position. Your local eligible trade union representative at Aarhus University negotiates your salary on your behalf. Other terms of employment Further information on the appointment procedure can be found in the Ministerial Order on the Appointment of Academic Staff at Universities . The appointment is in accordance with the Danish Confederation of Professional Associations (Akademikerne). Remuneration is in accordance with the above, and the Salary agreement catalogue for staff at Health . Researchers recruited from abroad are offered a special researcher tax scheme with a lower tax rate. Further information on qualification requirements and job description can be found in the Ministerial order on Job Structure for Academic Staff at Universities . Application – Associate professor Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - associate professor A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios The five publications stated as the most important in the 'Template for applicant - associate professor' must be submitted Research plan, which is part of the 'Template to applicant - associate professor' can be uploaded separately (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the e-recruitment system (optional) Application – Assistant professor Your application must include the following: Motivated application Curriculum Vitae Diploma Template for applicant - assistant professor or tenure track assistant professor A list of publications A teaching portfolio. We refer to Guideline on the use of teaching portfolios A maximum of five of the publications of greatest relevance to the job may be submitted (optional) Research plan, which is part of the 'Template to applicant - assistant professor or tenure track assistant professor' can be uploaded separately (optional) Coauthor statement(s) can be uploaded (optional) References/recommendations can be uploaded separately in the e-recruitment system (optional) We refer to the faculty’s Guidelines for applicants . Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. International applicant? Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including assistance with relocation and career counselling to expat partners. Please find more information about the International Staff Office and the range of services here . Aarhus University also has a Junior Researcher Association and offers career development support. You can read more about these resources here . The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website . Questions about the position\n                            ? Bjarni Jóhann Vilhjálmsson Professor, Department of Public Health - National Centre for Register-based Research Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Health Administrative Centre - Health HR +4593521835 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Sunday 11 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/scientific-writer-and-communicator-from-cutting-edge-science-to-a-sustainable-biodiverse-future": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Scientific writer and communicator: from cutting-edge science to a sustainable biodiverse future - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity Scientific writer and communicator: from cutting-edge science to a sustainable biodiverse future Deadline 12 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity Ny Munkegade 114 8000 Aarhus C Fixed term full-time position 1 Jan 2027 - 31 Dec 2028 ID: 168892 Apply Are you passionate about transforming cutting-edge science into compelling stories that inspire action and understanding? Do you enjoy communicating complex research in ways that engage scientists, decision-makers, and the wider public? Are you an exceptional writer with a talent for storytelling, scientific editing, and creative communication across multiple platforms? Then join us in telling the story of how science can help shape a sustainable future for biodiversity and society. The Department of Biology invites applications for a Scientific Writer and Communications Specialist position. Start date: 1st of January 2027. You will join the Center for Sustainable Landscapes Under Global Change (SustainScapes) and work closely with Professor Signe Normand and the centre’s researchers to bring groundbreaking research to life. The centre investigates how biodiversity and landscapes are changing under global environmental pressures and develops new knowledge to support a more sustainable future. As part of our team, you will help communicate the centre's scientific discoveries, visions, and impacts to diverse audiences. You will play a central role in translating complex scientific results into clear, engaging, and visually appealing stories that showcase the significance of our research and its relevance for society. Your tasks will include: Leading the development of an inspiring coffee table book highlighting the centre's scientific journey, discoveries, people, and impacts. Assisting researchers in editing and refining scientific manuscripts, reports, and policy briefs. Working closely with researchers to identify and communicate emerging discoveries and societal impacts. Creating compelling stories around scientific breakthroughs, field campaigns, technological innovations, and interdisciplinary collaborations. Communicating research findings through interviews, news articles, visual storytelling, and multimedia content. Developing engaging content for the centre's website, newsletters, and social media channels. Expected start date and duration of employment The post position is expected to start 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible hereafter. Job description We are looking for a creative and proactive communicator who combines scientific insight with exceptional writing skills. You should enjoy working in a dynamic research environment and be motivated by helping excellent science reach and inspire a broader audience. This position offers a unique opportunity to contribute to an internationally ambitious research centre at the forefront of biodiversity science, sustainable landscapes, and global change research. Together, we aim not only to advance scientific knowledge but also to communicate that knowledge in ways that inspire engagement, understanding, and positive change. Qualifications You hold a Master's degree in a relevant field, such as biology, ecology, environmental science and/or has several years of experience with scientific writing and communication. A PhD within the mentioned fields is considered an advantage. Specifically, we look for the following qualifications: Outstanding writing and editing skills in English, with the ability to communicate complex scientific concepts clearly and accurately to diverse audiences. Demonstrated experience in scientific writing, communication, journalism, publishing, or related activities. Excellent ability to identify, develop, and communicate engaging narratives from scientific research. Experience editing scientific manuscripts, reports, grant applications, or other research-related documents. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to collaborate closely with researchers from different disciplines. Excellent organizational and project management skills, including the ability to coordinate multiple communication activities and deadlines simultaneously. A proactive, independent, and solution-oriented working style. At the center, we value a collegial and inclusive working environment and place strong emphasis on good relationships between colleagues and students. We therefore seek candidates with an open, constructive, and collaborative approach The Department of Biology The Department of Biology ( http://bio.au.dk/ ) provides a framework for research and teaching in all major biological subdisciplines. The department is especially known for its research contribution in biodiversity, global change and conservation biology, aquatic biology and ecology, arctic environment and ecosystems, genetics and evolution, microbiology and zoophysiology. The department currently encompasses approximately 200 academic and technical staff and 45 PhD students while 400 students follow the BSc and MSc programs offered by the institute. The Department offers a vibrant and informal research environment with a long-standing tradition of collaboration with international university partners. Much of the internal communication is in English and we welcome international applicants. What we offer an exciting interdisciplinary environment with many national, international and industrial collaborators a work environment encouraging lively, open and critical discussion a work environment with close working relationships, networking and social activities a workplace characterised by professionalism, equality and a healthy work-life balance Place of work and area of employment The place of employment is Aarhus University, Department of Biology, Section for Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity (ECOINF), Ny Munkegade 114, DK-8000 Aarhus C, Denmark, and the area of employment is Aarhus University with affiliated institutions. The work involves some periods of field work away from Aarhus. Contact information For further information, please contact: Prof. Signe Normand, +45 93 52 28 77, [email protected] . Deadline Applications must be received no later than 12 October 2026 Formalities and salary range Salary and terms as agreed between the Danish Ministry of Taxation and the Confederation of Professional Unions. You will be employed as an academic staff member at basic salary grades 4–8. Aarhus University’s ambition is to be an attractive and inspiring workplace for all and to foster a culture in which each individual has opportunities to thrive, achieve and develop. We view equality and diversity as assets, and we welcome all applicants. The application must be submitted via Aarhus University’s recruitment system, which can be accessed under the job advertisement on Aarhus University's website. Questions about the position\n                            ? Signe Normand Professor, Department of Biology - Ecoinformatics and Biodiversity +4593522877 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Nat-Tech Administrative Centre - Nat-Tech HR +4522521469 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Monday 12 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
    },
    "https://international.au.dk/about/profile/vacant-positions/job/postdoctoral-position-diversifying-embodiment-in-education-overcoming-inequity-in-embodied-learning-in-danish-vocational-education-and-beyond": {
      "title": "Questions about the position ?",
      "description": "Postdoctoral position: Diversifying Embodiment in Education: Overcoming Inequity in Embodied Learning in Danish Vocational Education and Beyond - Vacancy at Aarhus University Aarhus University logo For students For PhDs For employees Departments and faculties AU Library Brightspace MYSTUDIES.AU.DK Common pages for PhD students phd.au.dk PhD-portals Employee portals Find the local staff portal for your department or division. Departments and faculties Education International admissions at AU Bachelor's degree programmes Master's degree programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Meet AU Ask about studies Chat with our students International student blog Fairs and events On-demand webinars AU in many languages How to apply Bachelor’s degree programmes Master’s degree programmes Exchange programmes Summer University PhD programmes Education Research Research units and facilities Departments and schools Research centres Research facilities Research support Find a researcher PhD studies PhD at AU PhD programmes Vacant PhD positions How to apply Career services Researcher positions Vacant positions Fellowship programme at AIAS Attractive working conditions How to apply Funding opportunities Junior Researcher services Junior Researcher Association Campus resources Career services Research Life in Denmark Danish way of living Cost of living Family and work-life balance The Danes Learning and teaching culture Your new city Aarhus and surroundings Things to do in Aarhus Getting around AU locations For students Prepare your arrival Student housing Student life Future and career For PhDs and researchers Before arrival On arrival Housing Relocation and onboarding services Life in Denmark Collaboration How to collaborate with AU Business Collaboration Innovation and entrepreneurship Partner universities International alliances Technology Transfer at AU Commercialisation Technologies for licensing Spinouts Collaboration About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU Rankings Key statistics Strategy History Organisation Faculties Departments and schools Research centres Management Contact AU contact information and map AU locations Employee contact information International services Vacant positions Vacant positions Vacant PhD positions About AU You are here: AU About AU Profile Vacant positions About AU Profile Top reasons to choose AU AU in the world rankings Vacant positions Rules and Regulations Key statistics and annual reports Strategy Sustainability A changing campus History Alumni Honorary awards and grants Organisation AU for visitors Contact Danish School of Education - Education Studies, Emdrup - Faculty: Arts Postdoctoral position: Diversifying Embodiment in Education: Overcoming Inequity in Embodied Learning in Danish Vocational Education and Beyond Deadline 13 Oct 23:59 CEST Expected start 1 Jan Danish School of Education - Education Studies, Emdrup Tuborgvej 164 2400 København NV Full-time position ID: 168244 Apply The Danish School of Education (DPU) at the Faculty of Arts, Aarhus University, invites applications for a postdoctoral position within the research project Diversifying Embodiment in Education: Overcoming Inequity in Embodied Learning in Danish Vocational Education and Beyond , funded by Independent Research Fund Denmark. This is a full-time (37 hours per week), fixed-term (24 months) postdoctoral position, starting on 1 January 2027 and ending on 31 December 2028. The position is based within the Department of Education Studies at DPU’s campus in Emdrup, although the successful applicant should expect some travel activity between the department’s sites in Aarhus and Copenhagen. The Danish School of Education is committed to diversity and encourages all qualified applicants to apply, regardless of their personal background. Given the project’s empirical focus on educational settings in Denmark, applicants must be sufficiently proficient in the Danish language to conduct fieldwork and actively participate in these settings. Research Context The postdoctoral research will form part of the broader research project Diversifying Embodiment in Education: Overcoming Inequity in Embodied Learning in Danish Vocational Education and Beyond . The project addresses a key issue in contemporary education: how educational practices cultivate, recognise, and value certain bodies and sensory experiences in learning contexts while marginalising others, and how these processes contribute to educational inequities. Combining perspectives from educational studies, anthropology, embodiment studies, and critical disability studies, the project examines how different bodies and sensoria (i.e., culturally shaped ways of sensing and engaging with the world) are formed and valued through the corporeal, social, and material interactions that shape embodied learning. Danish vocational education and training (VET) serves as the empirical case. Within VET, students move between classrooms, workshops, and workplaces as apprentices, developing skills through interactions with teachers, peers, colleagues, technologies, tools, and materials. The project’s methodology draws on ethnographic and sensory approaches to examine the corporeal, social, and material dynamics that shape embodied learning across four vocational programmes (to be selected in collaboration with the postdoctoral researcher). The project comprises three subprojects. The postdoctoral researcher will collaborate with the Principal Investigator on one of these subprojects, examining interactions among students, teachers, and nonhuman actors within the four selected vocational programmes. In addition, the postdoctoral researcher will lead a second subproject focused on interactions among apprentices, workplace instructors, and nonhuman actors in workplace settings. Based on approximately three months of ethnographic fieldwork in workplaces associated with the four selected vocational programmes, this subproject will explore how instructors introduce new apprentices to embodied practices and perceptions of their future profession, and how they evaluate apprentices’ attempts to engage with these practices and perceptions. Particular attention will be paid to the role of nonhuman actors, such as materials, tools, assistive devices, and instructions, and to how apprentices and instructors interact with and through them. The position offers a unique opportunity to contribute to emerging international debates on embodiment, diversity, and educational inequality, and to advancing innovative theoretical and methodological approaches to the study of embodied learning. Further information Applicants are encouraged to contact project manager Maia Ebsen ( [email protected] ) for further information about the project. Postdoctoral position The selected applicant will work in close collaboration with the project manager, Maia Ebsen, throughout the period of employment. This will include planning and conducting fieldwork, processing and analysing empirical material, and organising seminars with the project’s advisory board. In addition to conducting and completing two subprojects, one in collaboration with the PI and one independently, the postdoctoral researcher is expected to: Actively engage in collaboration as a key part of the research project Publish one single-author peer-reviewed article) and one peer-reviewed article co-authored with the PI in relevant international journals Initiate, co-organise and participate in dissemination and knowledge exchange activities with the PI (e.g. conferences, seminars and other relevant academic events) Undertake a research visit of minimum two months at a relevant research institution outside Denmark Help disseminate research findings to wider audiences Contribute more broadly to the academic environment at the Department of Education Studies, establishing and maintaining good working relationships with both colleagues and students Teaching and supervision As a postdoctoral researcher, your position is primarily research-based but will also involve a limited amount of teaching and supervision. To that end, the selected applicant will be expected to participate in teaching and supervision activities and teach and supervise at BA and MA levels at the Department of Education Studies. Any teaching activities will be agreed with the head of department and/or the project PI. Qualifications Applicants should be able to document: solid experience in planning, conducting, analysing and disseminating ethnographic fieldwork an internationally oriented research profile, as documented by a PhD dissertation and/or research publications experience in independently managing research projects relevant teaching experience at university level commensurate with the length of the applicant’s academic career good communication and interpersonal skills for engaging productively with external organisations and institutions Furthermore, applicants who can document the following will have an advantage: a basic understanding of the Danish VET system a track record of research publications of a high international standard commensurate with the length of the applicant’s academic career experience of participating in national and international research networks time spent working at internationally recognised research institutions outside Denmark Applicants must also speak and write English at an advanced academic level. The application must be submitted in English. It must include a cover letter, a CV (including a list of publications), publications if relevant to the position (maximum two), and a teaching statement (or equivalent documentation of teaching experience and qualifications). Moreover, applicants are asked to include a research statement (approximately two pages). This should include a description of how their previous research experience and qualifications are relevant to the position, and a proposal for research to be undertaken within the framework for the research project outlined above, outlining a methodological and conceptual approach for the subproject led by the postdoc. A timetable should be included for completing this subproject within the period of employment. Lastly, the application must include a copy of the applicant’s PhD degree certificate or other documentation confirming the expected date of degree completion. Please note that applications lacking such documentation will not be considered. Although the Aarhus University application system includes an option to upload letters of recommendation, applicants should not include letters of recommendation or references with the application. Applicants who are invited to an interview may be asked to provide references. For further information about the position, please contact Miriam Madsen , head of the Department of Education Studies, email: [email protected] , tel.: +4520842672 Read about the Department of Education Studies here . About the Danish School of Education The Danish School of Education at Aarhus University is Denmark’s largest centre for research and teaching in the fields of education, didactics and learning, with approximately 240 full-time researchers, including 80 PhD students, and 4,500 Bachelor’s and Master’s degree students. The school’s activities are characterised by a high degree of interdisciplinarity and close interaction with society, including businesses, organisations, government agencies and institutions, both in Denmark and abroad. International applicants International applicants are encouraged to read about family and work-life balance as well as the attractive working conditions and other benefits of working at Aarhus University and in Denmark, including healthcare, paid holidays and, if relevant, maternity/paternity leave, childcare and schooling. Aarhus University offers a broad variety of services for international researchers and accompanying families, including a relocation service and career counselling for expat partners. For information about taxation, see the taxation aspects of international researchers’ employment by AU . Please find more information about entering and working in Denmark here: http://international.au.dk/research/ Aarhus University also offers a junior researcher development programme targeted at career development for postdocs at AU. You can read more about it here: https://talent.au.dk/junior-researcher-development-programme/ Questions about the position\n                            ? Miriam Madsen Associate Professor, Danish School of Education - Education Studies, Aarhus +4520842672 [email protected] Questions about application and proces\n                            ? Arts Administrative Centre - HR, AR, Emdrup +4593508310 [email protected] Apply Deadline: \n                            Tuesday 13 Oct 2026 at 23:59 CEST 37,500 students including PhD students 9,000 employees full-time equivalents 1,2 EUR billion in revenue Working at AU Key information for potential and future international employees AIAS-Fellowships See deadline and call for applications for programmes at Aarhus Institute of Advanced Studies (AIAS) PhD positions at Aarhus University See a full list of vacant PhD positions at Aarhus University How to apply for academic positions Revised\n    30.06.2026 - AU HR Aarhus University Nordre Ringgade 1 8000 Aarhus E-mail: [email protected] Tel: +45 8715 0000 CVR no: 31119103 EORI no: DK-31119103 About us Profile Departments and faculties Contact & map Vacant positions Degree programmes Bachelor's programmes Master’s programmes Engineering programmes Exchange programmes AU Summer University PhD programmes Shortcuts Library For students For PhDs For employees For Alumni © — Cookies at au.dk Privacy Policy Accessibility Statement 96261 / i48"
